<!-- One element of a SLICED payload: the codec `msg_put` repeats when its
     extension chain carries the shared-memory marker.

     Upstream lays a Put's payload out one of two ways. Without the marker
     it is a length and that many bytes. With it, the payload is a count of
     slices followed by that many elements, and each element is a kind, a
     length and that many bytes (`commons/zenoh-codec/src/core/zbuf.rs` @
     `impl<W> WCodec<&ZBuf, &mut W> for Zenoh080Sliced<$bound>`). Two kinds
     share the one element shape, which is why one codec is enough
     (`commons/zenoh-codec/src/core/zbuf.rs` @ `const RAW: u8 = 0;` and
     `const SHM_PTR: u8 = 1;`):

       kind 0  RAW      the bytes are payload bytes, as an unsliced Put
                        would have carried them.
       kind 1  SHM_PTR  the bytes are a serialized shared-memory buffer
                        descriptor (`ShmBufInfo`), not payload: the payload
                        lives in the segment the descriptor names.

     `kind` is a variable-length integer read at the width upstream reads
     it. Upstream reads it through the sliced codec's bounded-u32 codec and
     keeps the low byte (`commons/zenoh-codec/src/core/zbuf.rs` @
     `let kind: u8 = self.codec.read(&mut *reader)?;`), and that codec
     refuses a value wider than 32 bits (`commons/zenoh-codec/src/core/zint.rs` @
     `if (x & !(<$bound>::MAX as u64)) != 0 {`). So the field is 32 bits wide
     here, which refuses what upstream refuses, and the host takes its low
     byte, which keeps what upstream keeps. A writer produces one byte for
     the two kinds there are (0 and 1); a decoder that meets any other low
     byte has met a kind this node does not know, and the host refuses it
     rather than guessing.

     `bytes` carries `sce:max-size="256"` like the unsliced `payload` it
     replaces: the no-alloc owned projection keeps that as its inline
     capacity and the alloc projection is unbounded, so a RAW slice is no
     tighter than the payload was. A descriptor is far shorter than that.

     Imported by `msg_put.scxml`, which gates its two layouts on the
     extension chain with `extensions.has(0x2)` (SCE's chain-membership
     predicate) and repeats this element `slice_count` times. -->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       sce:kind="codec" sce:default-endian="big" name="zbuf_slice">
  <datamodel>
    <sce:field id="kind"  sce:type="uint32" sce:byte="0" sce:bit-size="vle"/>
    <sce:field id="len"   sce:type="uint64" sce:byte="1" sce:bit-size="vle"/>
    <sce:field id="bytes" sce:type="bytes"  sce:byte="2" sce:bit-size="length-ref"
               sce:length-field="len" sce:max-size="256"/>
  </datamodel>
</scxml>
